Q: How To Add CSS3 Transition With HTML5 details/summary tag reveal? A: <p>Here is a version which combines different elements:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Browser compatibility (~96%) — Does not use <code>::details-content</code> or <code>interpolate-size</code></p>
</li>
<li><p>Closing transition — That's why we need JS</p>
</li>
<li><p>No latency — Using <code>height</code> instead of <code>max-height</code> to prevent lag</p>
</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>It requires to wrap the contents inside elements (like <code>div</code>) to calculate the item height.</p>
<p>Also, for a real life application, screen resizing should be handled</p>
</blockquote>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>document.querySelectorAll('details').forEach((details) =&gt; {
  const summary = details.querySelector('summary')
  summary.addEventListener('click', (evt) =&gt; {
    if (!details.open) {
      // OPENING =&gt; No need to prevent default browser behavior
      // Set the 
      details.style.height = `${Array.from(details.children).reduce(
        (height, child) =&gt; height + outerHeight(child), 0
      )}px`
    } else {
      evt.preventDefault()
      details.style.height = ''
      // CLOSING =&gt; Wait for the end of the transition
      details.addEventListener('transitionend', () =&gt; {
        details.open = false
      }, { once: true })
    }
  })
})

// Get element full height including margins
function outerHeight(el) {
  const style = getComputedStyle(el);
  return el.getBoundingClientRect().height 
    + parseFloat(style.marginTop) + parseFloat(style.marginBottom);
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>details {
  overflow: hidden;
  height: 1em;
  transition: height .4s ease-in-out;
}
summary {
  cursor: pointer;
}

/* theming */
details {
  margin: 10px auto;
  padding: 10px;
  border: 2px solid grey;
  border-radius: 5px;
  width: 50%;
}
details:open {
  border-color: black;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;details&gt;
  &lt;summary&gt;Item 1&lt;/summary&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;
    Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed 
    do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. 
    Ut enim ad minim veniam.
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/details&gt;
&lt;details&gt;
  &lt;summary&gt;Item 2&lt;/summary&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;
    Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum
    dolore eu fugiat nulla pariatur.
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;
    Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat
    non proident, sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id
    est laborum. Quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut 
    aliquip ex ea commodo consequat.
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/details&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Connection error via express app A: <p>The simplest way I can think of to fix it would be to put each button in a separate form that each has a separate dimension set in it.  Then, the dimension you get in the form post would be the only the one for the button that was pressed.</p>

<p>My handlebars might be a bit rusty, but perhaps something like this:</p>

<pre><code>{{#each rows}}

    &lt;form method="post" action="/delivery/chooseBoxSelected"&gt;
        &lt;input type="hidden" name="userHashtag" value="{{userHashtag}}"&gt;
        &lt;input type="hidden" name="boxSelectedValue" value="{{this.box_id}}"&gt;
        &lt;input type="hidden" name="boxSelectedDimension" value="{{this.longestDimension}}"&gt;

        &lt;button class="btn-dimension" type="submit"&gt;
            &lt;i class="fa fa-cube" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
            &amp;nbsp;Longest dimension {{this.longestDimension}}"
        &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
{{/each}}  
</code></pre>

<p>And, you may have to adjust the CSS to deal with the fact that this is now N separate forms (one for each button).</p>
